## Why This Is Asked
This question tests your ability to design a multi-tenant streaming analytics pipeline that addresses several complex requirements: cross-region data residency enforcement, privacy masking, tenant isolation, lineage tracking, rollback capabilities, schema evolution, partner data sharing, and operational observability—all working together in a unified system.

## Key Concepts
- **Pub/Sub streaming** with Dataflow for real-time processing
- **Per-tenant BigQuery** partitions with US/EU residency enforcement
- **Centralized policy engine** for consistent masking across all tenants
- **Data Catalog lineage** for governance and auditability
- **BigQuery Data Exchange** for secure cross-region partner sharing
- **24-hour rollback** via replay from Pub/Sub snapshots
- **Schema evolution** with managed schema registry
- **Observability** with retries, dead-letter queues, and alerting

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: policy-based masking
function mask(record, policy) {
  const r = {...record};
  for (const field of policy.fields) {
    if (field.mask) r[field.name] = maskValue(r[field.name], field.maskType);
  }
  return r;
}

function maskValue(value, type) {
  switch (type) {
    case 'hash': return crypto.createHash('sha256').update(value).digest('hex');
    case 'redact': return '[REDACTED]';
    case 'partial': return value.slice(0, 2) + '***';
    default: return value;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical data quality governance in a high-volume, multi-tenant streaming pipeline on GCP. It requires integrating Data Catalog for contracts and lineage, Dataflow for real-time checks, per-tenant isolation in BigQuery, and a robust rollback/testing strategy with synthetic data and anomaly detection. It also assesses cost and latency trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Data contracts and lineage via Data Catalog
- Real-time quality gates in Dataflow (schema drift, null checks, ranges)
- Per-tenant isolation in BigQuery datasets
- 24h per-tenant rollback and testing strategies
- Quarantine vs drop and anomaly alerting

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: Dataflow DoFn quality gate
class QualityGateDoFn extends DoFn {
  processElement(ctx) {
    const rec = JSON.parse(ctx.element);
    if (!validSchema(rec) || hasNulls(outFields(rec))) {
      quarantine(rec);
      return;
    }
    emit(rec);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test a schema drift event across tenants without data leakage?
- What metrics and SLAs tie to 24h rollbacks and data contracts?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical knowledge of end-to-end streaming ingestion on GCP with privacy and governance considerations. It evaluates the candidate's ability to apply core GCP primitives—including Pub/Sub, Dataflow, BigQuery, and Data Catalog—while addressing real-world concerns like multi-tenancy, data quality, and operational resilience.

## Key Concepts
- **Streaming Ingestion**: Pub/Sub for real-time event capture, Dataflow for scalable processing
- **Per-Tenant Isolation**: Shared raw table architecture with Authorized Views for secure multi-tenancy
- **Data Governance**: Data Catalog for schema registry and lineage tracking
- **Data Quality**: Schema enforcement, null validation, and statistical anomaly detection
- **Operational Concerns**: Late-arrival handling, rollback procedures, and schema evolution

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton DoFn to compute 3-sigma anomalies
class AnomalyDetectionDoFn extends DoFn {
  processElement(element) {
    const mean = this.meanField; // Pre-computed per tenant
    const stdDev = this.stdDevField;
    const threshold = 3 * stdDev;
    const value = element.numericField;
    const isAnomaly = Math.abs(value - mean) > threshold;
    // Output with anomaly flag
  }
}
```

